Commit, merge, and close finished work. The input is a worktree path and an
optional backlog slug — from {{ARGS}}, or from the skill that delegated
here. Pass a slug, not a list number. Every gate below stops and waits for
the user — never collapse two gates into one approval, and never skip one
for being mid-pipeline or in an unattended run. Run the gate's diff and the
commit in that worktree (`git -C <worktree> ...`), never in whatever
checkout you happen to be in.

## 1. Gate: commit
**Slug-guard callout.** Before requesting commit approval, run
`python3 {{TOOLKIT_SCRIPTS}}/dev_status.py slug-check --repo <worktree> --slug <slug>`
(omit `--slug` on an ad-hoc commit with no backlog item) — the no-slugs
rule in enforced form: it scans the staged diff's added lines for the
item's resolved slug and any other backlog-store id. Any nonzero exit
blocks approval. Exit 1 prints `<file>:<line>: <id>` findings and the
commit is refused: fix the diff, or — only for a genuine non-slug string —
re-run with `--allow <file>:<line>:<id> <reason>` (one per finding; the
reason is recorded), and surface each override with its reason in the
approval prompt. Exit 2 (store, git, or parse errors) also blocks
approval — never treat an error as clean. The check must be the last
thing run before `git commit`: if anything is staged after it, re-run it.
If a `--allow` override was applied and the subsequent `git commit` is then
refused by a pre-commit hook that re-runs slug-check without per-finding
overrides (the `githooks-global` hook), commit with `git commit --no-verify`
— the override is already recorded by this gate's `--allow` run. `--no-verify`
also skips the repo's other pre-commit checks (generated-doc staleness, the
seed guard), so only use it after confirming those are satisfied, and surface
the override and the bypass in the approval prompt. Do not use `--no-verify`
when no hook refuses the commit.

{{COMMIT_GATE_BODY}} The proposed commit is the staged tree: review
`git status` and `git diff --cached` (`--stat` first when it runs past ~50
lines), not only an unstaged `git diff`, before
requesting approval. Show the `review-diff` report (reviewer line and triage
table) with the diff. `review-diff` did not run on this staged tree? Say so
plainly at the gate — "review-diff did not run" — and do not run it
silently from here.

**Contract-touch callout.** If the item's `acceptance` criteria cite contract
files (`refs[].path`), check whether this staged tree touches any of them:
run `python3 {{TOOLKIT_SCRIPTS}}/dev_status.py contract-touch <slug> --repo <path>`
(NUL-delimited `git diff --cached --name-status -z -M -C` under the hood, so
unusual filenames and both sides of renames/copies survive; JSON output) or
`--range <base>..<head>` for the whole branch. If it reports matches, call
them out explicitly as contract changes needing the reviewer's attention. A
rename whose *old* path matched means the acceptance refs now point at the
old path — they need an operator `--operator-override` update, never a silent
follow.

## 2. Gate: commit-then-land
{{LAND_BODY}}

**`git worktree remove` fails with "Directory not empty"?** A dev server
(or other long-running process) launched against this worktree during
verification — e.g. via the `run` skill's smoke-check pattern — can outlive the port
kill that pattern documents: killing the port's listener doesn't always
reap a wrapper's child process (a `bun run dev` parent whose `node .../next
dev` child keeps the worktree as its cwd). `git worktree remove` then fails
partway, and can strip the worktree's git-admin metadata (it drops out of
`git worktree list`) while leaving the directory on disk — a second
`remove` won't find it. Find what's still holding it open with `lsof +D
<worktree-path>`, `kill` those exact PIDs (never a broad `pkill -f` — it
can match unrelated processes, including the agent's own), then remove the
orphaned directory directly (`rm -rf <worktree-path>`) and retry
`git branch -d`.

## 3. Close
No slug? Stop here — there is no backlog item to close.

{{CLOSE_BODY}}
